throughout the debut season of 1883  isabelle may's character elsa dutton  serves as one of the show's biggest  cornerstones not only does the young  dutton ancestor quickly establish  herself as a key protagonist in the  yellowstone prequel spin-off but she  also provides the narration for the  taylor sheridan created show one of the  most intriguing qualities about elsa is  her heartiness and her independence  traits handed down from her parents  james and margaret dutton played by tim  mcgraw and faith hill throughout season  1 of 1883 elsa often finds herself in  some of the show's toughest situations  as she her family and other settlers  made their way from texas to oregon in  hopes of a new life through every  potentially disastrous or fatal  encounter the duttons pulled through as  a family unit with elsa providing  audiences a unique window in their  mental and emotional headspace looking  back it's easy to pick out several  different instances and moments from  1883 where fans were genuinely upset or  affected by what elsa was going through  however there's one scene in particular  that firmly stands out above the rest  with some viewers calling it almost  unbearable to watch the scene in  question comes in the final moments of  the show's season finale elsa is  preparing to say her final goodbyes as  her condition has become increasingly  worse what happens next is a moment that  1883 and yellowstone fans will likely  never forget a majority of the finale  episode is spent trying to get her the  medical care elsa desperately needs even  though it's clear the wound is too  severe she's not long for this world a  heartbreaking decision is made james  dutton will travel with elsa and  wherever the pair stop to rest is the  place where the dutton patriarch will  lay down roots for his family's new  homestead during elsa's final moments  james takes his daughter to an open area  and they rest by a very specific tree a  tree that yellowstone fans quickly  recognized she tells her father this is  the spot let's just sit here rest for a  bit it's a poignant moment and one  that's also extremely heartbreaking to  the point where viewers were genuinely  upset and angry with paramount for  airing it elsa's death helps set up the  backstory for how the dutton family  ranch came to settle in paradise valley  montana the location where the  present-day dutton family lives works  and schemes the untimely passing of  james eldest child is unexpected and  perhaps not the kind of finale plot  twist fans were hoping to see it feels  like a cruel misdirect having elsa  taking on such an important position as  a narrator and becoming a central figure  only to be wiped off the board by  something as tradable as an arrow wound  even by 1883 standards and yet there is  something moving and knowing that it is  elsa a character audiences spend so much  time with in season one who will be the  metaphorical foundation on which dutton  yellowstone ranch is built although it's  clear to see how elsa's death actually  served to move the plot of 1883 as well  as the saga of the dutton family forward  fans were not pleased to see the beloved  character or actress isabelle may exit  the show especially in such a tragic  manner one user tweeted since elsa died  i cancelled another expressed quote i  can't live without her posting a  petition online to request that  paramount network bring may's character  back somehow their disgust flowed over  into a second tweet which included this  observation  elsa had to die because it explains how  they end up in montana how stupid  elsewhere on twitter one fan explained  quote it's just too heartbreaking and  another said simply so many tears over  on youtube viewers were just as upset  one user commented that final scene was  heartbreaking and almost unbearable  another youtuber remarked won't be the  same without elsa i can now cancel  paramount plus the death of elsa was a  moment that not only weighed heavy on  viewers but the actors as well 1883 star  tim mcgraw told variety we literally  couldn't read the final scripts to each  other because faith hill and i were  crying so much i mean ugly boo hoo  crying i was a blubbering idiot  check out one of our newest videos right  here plus even more looper videos about  yellowstone and 1883 are coming soon  subscribe to our youtube channel and hit  the bell so you don't miss a single one
 

